CONCEPT: A system of one circle and one line has zero, one, or two solutions, shown side by side as three fixed cases; which case occurs is decided by comparing the center-to-line distance with the radius (equivalently, the sign of the discriminant).
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): Comparing the center-to-line distance with the radius — equivalently, the sign of the discriminant after substitution — completely determines whether a circle-line system has zero, one, or two solutions.
DEMO STEPS: 6 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

